CAT = "nzfkCapacity"; SEG = "CAP"
# Load plate, rated capacity, load centre, load calculation and derating (NZQA 40735 and 40736 operating parameters).
Q = [
("Where does an operator find a forklift's rated capacity?",
 'Load plate', ['Fuel or charge gauge', "Owner's service logbook", 'Sticker on the seat back'],
 'Skill standard 40735 defines the load plate (load capacity data plate) as the placard on every forklift giving its configuration, design and limits, including rated capacity.'),
('What must an operator check before lifting, according to skill standard 40735?',
 'Load weight against the plate', ['Fuel level against the gauge', 'Tyre pressure against the door', 'Mast height against the roof'],
 "40735's practical evidence starts with a correct check of the load plate against the load weight, so the weight is within the forklift's rated capacity."),
('Two pallets weigh the same, one 800 mm deep and one 1,200 mm deep, both even. Which has the longer load centre?',
 'The 1,200 mm deep pallet', ['The 800 mm deep pallet', 'Both have the same', 'Neither has a load centre'],
 "An even load's centre of gravity sits halfway along its depth, so 1,200 mm deep gives a 600 mm load centre and 800 mm deep gives 400 mm. The longer load centre cuts capacity more."),
("A load sits further out on the forks than the plate's load centre. What happens to safe capacity?",
 'It drops', ['It rises', 'It stays the same', 'It doubles'],
 'A longer load centre gives the load a longer lever arm about the front wheels, so the same weight tips the truck sooner and the safe capacity falls.'),
('A truck is rated 2,500 kg at a 500 mm load centre. Using the simple moment ratio, about what is it good for at 625 mm?',
 '2,000 kg', ['2,500 kg', '3,125 kg', '1,500 kg'],
 'Capacity falls in proportion to the load centre: 2,500 x 500 / 625 = 2,000 kg. 3,125 kg comes from multiplying by the ratio the wrong way round.'),
('A 1,800 kg rated truck (500 mm centre) must lift a long crate whose centre is 750 mm out. About what can it take by the moment ratio?',
 '1,200 kg', ['1,800 kg', '2,700 kg', '900 kg'],
 '1,800 x 500 / 750 = 1,200 kg. Multiplying the other way gives 2,700 kg, which would overload the truck.'),
("Which of these lowers a forklift's actual capacity?",
 'Lifting to a greater height', ['Lowering the load to travel', 'Centring the load on the forks', 'Tilting the mast back'],
 'Many load plates and charts give lower capacity at greater lift heights; 40735 notes rated capacity depends on load centre, lift height, attachment weight and machine condition.'),
('What is derating?',
 'Cutting capacity for an attachment', ['Raising capacity for solid tyres', 'Slowing the truck in narrow aisles', 'Lowering the mast for doorways'],
 "Fitting an attachment needs a reduction in the truck's actual capacity, called derating. 40735 and 40736 list the principle of derating among the operating parameters."),
("Why does an attachment cut a forklift's capacity?",
 'It adds mass further forward', ['It makes the engine weaker', 'It shortens the fork length', 'It stiffens the mast chains'],
 "An attachment has its own weight and usually pushes the load's centre of gravity further forward, so less load can be carried before the truck tips."),
('After an attachment is fitted, which capacity figure applies?',
 'Derated figure for that set-up', ['Original figure on the plate', "The attachment's own weight", 'Heaviest pallet on the site'],
 'A new capacity plate for the truck with the attachment should be fitted before use. The original plate is for the bare forks only.'),
("What does an attachment's own load plate tell the operator?",
 'Its rated capacity and weight', ['Its paint colour and maker', 'When the operator last trained', "The shift's speed limit"],
 "Skill standard 40736 says attachments typically have their own capacity data plate giving the attachment's rated capacity, weight and effect on the forklift's overall capacity."),
('Who should work out the derating for a new attachment?',
 'Maker or authorised supplier', ['Any operator on that shift', "The site's newest trainee", 'The delivery driver'],
 'Derating should only be done by someone with the right knowledge and experience, in consultation with the manufacturer or authorised supplier.'),
("A load's weight is unknown and nothing is marked on it. What should the operator do?",
 'Find the weight before lifting', ['Lift it slowly and see', 'Assume it matches the last pallet', 'Lift it with the mast tilted forward'],
 '40735 lists load calculation as a safety consideration. Lifting to test the weight risks tipping the truck; the weight must be known or worked out first.'),
('A pallet holds 24 cartons of 35 kg on a 25 kg pallet. What is the total weight?',
 '865 kg', ['840 kg', '725 kg', '875 kg'],
 '24 x 35 kg of cartons plus the 25 kg pallet: 840 + 25 = 865 kg. Forgetting the pallet leaves 840 kg.'),
('A loaded pallet weighs 640 kg. The pallet is 25 kg and each carton 41 kg. How many cartons are on it?',
 '15', ['16', '14', '12'],
 'Take off the pallet first: 640 - 25 = 615 kg of cartons, and 615 / 41 = 15.'),
("What does 'nominal capacity' on a forklift describe?",
 'Rating at the standard load centre', ['Weight of the truck when empty', "Truck's top travel speed", 'Highest lift height it reaches'],
 'Nominal (rated) capacity is the load the maker says the truck can lift at its standard load centre and lift height; actual capacity drops when those change.'),
('A load plate shows 3,000 kg at 500 mm and 2,400 kg at 3,500 mm lift height. A 2,700 kg load must go to 3,500 mm. What should happen?',
 'Do not lift it to that height', ['Lift it slowly to 3,500 mm', 'Lift it with the mast forward', 'Add weight to the counterweight'],
 'At 3,500 mm the plate allows 2,400 kg, so 2,700 kg is over capacity there. Adding counterweight is never allowed unless the manufacturer approves it.'),
("Why must the counterweight never be changed without the manufacturer's approval?",
 'Plate ratings assume it', ['It voids the paint warranty', 'It slows battery charging', 'It changes how the horn sounds'],
 "The capacity on the plate is worked out with the original counterweight. Adding weight or passengers to 'balance' a heavy load overloads the axle and mast."),
('On an electric forklift, why must the battery be the size and weight the maker specifies?',
 'The battery is counterweight', ['Lighter batteries charge too fast', 'Heavier batteries void the endorsement', 'The plate lists only battery voltage'],
 'On electric counterbalanced trucks the battery forms part of the counterweight, so a lighter battery reduces stability and the plate rating no longer holds.'),
('A load is placed off to one side of the forks. What is the main risk?',
 'Sideways instability', ['Faster battery drain', 'Higher lift speed', 'Fork tips bending upward'],
 'An off-centre load moves the combined centre of gravity to one side, which reduces lateral stability, especially when turning.'),
('What should an operator do before lifting a load in a stillage or cage marked with a weight?',
 'Compare it with the plate', ['Ignore it and weigh it later', 'Lift it and read the gauge', 'Ask another driver to guess'],
 'Using a marked weight lets the operator confirm the load is within the rated capacity at its load centre before any lift starts.'),
('A load is 1,100 mm long and sits evenly against the fork face. Where is its load centre?',
 '550 mm', ['1,100 mm', '275 mm', '750 mm'],
 'For an even load against the fork face, the centre of gravity is halfway along its length: 1,100 / 2 = 550 mm.'),
('A load 1,600 mm deep of even weight sits right back on the fork face. What is its load centre?',
 '800 mm', ['1,600 mm', '400 mm', '1,000 mm'],
 'Half of 1,600 mm is 800 mm, the load centre of an even load placed against the fork face.'),
('Fork extensions are fitted to carry long loads. What happens to capacity?',
 'It falls; a new rating applies', ['It rises with the longer tines', 'It is unchanged by the extension', 'It changes only in wet weather'],
 'Fork extensions are attachments: they add weight and move the load out, so capacity must be derated and a matching plate fitted.'),
("A truck's plate lists a 600 mm load centre. The operator's load is centred at 500 mm. Can the plate figure be used?",
 'Yes, within the rated distance', ['No, the load is too close in', 'Only if the mast is raised', 'Only on a downhill slope'],
 "A load centre shorter than the plate's gives a shorter lever arm, so the plate's rated capacity still applies (it is not increased beyond the plate)."),
('Why not just lift an unknown load to see if the truck copes?',
 'The truck can tip once it lifts', ['It wastes battery charge', 'It blocks the aisle for others', 'It wears the drive tyres'],
 'If the load is over capacity, the rear wheels can lift and the truck tip forward as the load leaves the ground. The weight must be known before the lift.'),
('Which piece of information is NOT normally on a forklift load plate?',
 "Operator's training date", ['Rated capacity of the truck', 'Rated load centre distance', 'Maximum lift height'],
 "The load plate gives the truck's configuration and limits: rated capacity, load centre and lift heights. Operator training records belong in the PCBU's records."),
('A load of 1,500 kg is within the plate, but the forks are only part-way under it. Why is that a problem?',
 'The load sits further out', ['The truck becomes lighter', 'The mast tilts by itself', 'The horn stops working'],
 "Forks only part-way in leave the load's centre further from the fork face, lengthening the load centre and cutting safe capacity."),
]
